  "interpretation": [
    "The trained ModernBERT result is extremely inexpensive because its 27 questions are fixed in learned heads; it is not an arbitrary-question system.",
    "GLiClass Modern Large preserves arbitrary labels in one uni-encoder pass and is simulated near the trained-head ModernBERT cost curve because both use a ModernBERT-large backbone.",
    "GLiClass Large preserves arbitrary labels but repeats the state across two label groups and has a 512-token context, so 256-token-overlap chunking raises its cost sharply for long states.",
    "Zero-shot ModernBERT preserves arbitrary hypotheses but repeats the state inside every NLI pair, creating a measured 28.67x token-compute amplification for 27 questions.",
    "JEV preserves arbitrary typed questions while billing as shared state plus incremental question tokens; under the stated assumptions, Noul becomes cheaper than zero-shot ModernBERT above approximately 586 state tokens.",
    "Sol and Luna use a standardized one-call prompt with fixed 27-label output; their curves include both input and output token charges but exclude unavailable hidden reasoning-token usage.",
    "The JEV API capacity required to sustain this hypothetical load was not tested; its cost is linearly extrapolated from published per-token pricing."
  ],
